# All planning happens in the root OpenSpec

Every OpenSpec change, whether it touches several clones or one, is planned, tracked and archived in the meta-repository's `openspec/` with sdd-kit in meta-repository mode. Only code and tests stay in the clones. Small edits can be made without OpenSpec. The reason: features usually touch several parts of the system, for example an API and its interface, and one agent with one set of specs sees them whole.

## Considered Options

**A separate OpenSpec in each repository, with the root only linking changes.** The requirements of one feature end up in several places, and a repository without OpenSpec has none at all.

**Mixed: shared changes in the root, single-repository changes in that repository.** Several places for the specs of one system, and it is unclear where to look for a requirement.

**Only the root OpenSpec (chosen).** One place for the requirements of every part.

## Consequences

- A clone's own `openspec/`, if it has one, is not changed from the root; sdd-kit forbids it. Moving its specs into the root, or removing it, is a clone backlog entry ([../clone-backlog.md](../clone-backlog.md)).
- The agent is started from the root, otherwise the sdd-kit hooks do not work.
- sdd-kit is installed once into the root and its files are committed, so every developer has the same hooks and checks.
